WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.733 --> 00:00:06.316
(chair creaks)

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.370
- [Mark] All right, Vern.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.230
Vern,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.063
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:14.063 --> 00:00:15.250
Where are you from originally?

00:00:21.451 --> 00:00:24.427
- I grew up in Crownpoint area

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:29.970
and (sniffs) Chinle and Many Farm area.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.750
- [Mark] And that's here in New Mexico?

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.733
- New Mexico and Arizona.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.240
- [Mark] And you're Navajo?

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.703
- I'm Navajo, full-blooded.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:41.643
- [Mark] Tell me about your childhood,

00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:44.640
you had both your parents
growing up as a kid?

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.473
- I had both of my parents

00:00:45.473 --> 00:00:48.010
as I was growing up as child, yes.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.373
- [Mark] How was your childhood?

00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:53.297
- Fair, mediocre some days,

00:00:53.297 --> 00:00:56.140
and some days I don't understand.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.133
- [Mark] How would you
describe your childhood?

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.370
- At times (mumbles)
I guess, I don't know.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.123
They (mumbles) with me.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:14.627
I just was out to wanna go find...

00:01:21.703 --> 00:01:22.640
I was curious.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.913
I'm a curious guy,

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.520
and that's how I grew up,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.713
having fun, playing hard,

00:01:31.278 --> 00:01:33.020
and just getting away from-

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.960
- [Mark] What was your relationship
like with your parents?

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.413
- Parents were like (clears throat)

00:01:39.686 --> 00:01:40.790
once I walked in the room,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.090
I know I was gonna be
disciplined, go wash up,

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.540
clean up, we're gonna go eat.

00:01:45.822 --> 00:01:46.822
And I remember that.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.530
I fall in line and do what I gotta do.

00:01:51.851 --> 00:01:53.400
Basically, I was a good boy.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.233
Good son.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.570
- [Mark] And then alcohol
came into the picture

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.033
for you at what age?

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.403
- About like probably 8 to 12 came in.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.950
Yeah, but the parents were
the ones who were drinking,

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:13.783
not me.

00:02:13.783 --> 00:02:15.527
I was like, "What the hell
is going on with this?

00:02:15.527 --> 00:02:17.200
"What is this about?"

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.803
I didn't know, I had no idea.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:20.720
All I know is that

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.050
I had friends up and I
look like I'm curious.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.300
I want to know, but watch myself,

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.880
I'll have to run or somethin'.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.410
I had fear in me,

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.113
and that basically took place for me,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.470
watch over my sisters and my brother.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.380
- [Mark] Did you finish high school?

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.307
- No.
- No.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.050
- No, I didn't finish high school,

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.170
but I wanted to and I always wanted to,

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.200
and I still want to,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.187
but then I got asked,

00:02:50.187 --> 00:02:51.903
"Ah, forget it man, what the,

00:02:51.903 --> 00:02:53.960
"it's just a piece of form."

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.450
Most of the people who
apply for a job anyway...

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.990
- [Mark] So today you're living
on the street here in Gallup

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.600
- Today, I was living on
the streets of Gallup.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.470
- [Mark] How long-
- for that time being.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.985
- [Mark] How long you've been homeless?

00:03:08.985 --> 00:03:13.985
- Ah, well over 20 years.

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:17.570
- [Mark] Twenty years, and
what is the drug you use most?

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.210
Is it alcohol?

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:23.140
- I use alcohol and yeah, alcohol,

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.100
tobacco, marijuana,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.833
and other drugs, I just
kinda put on the side.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.570
I don't use it that much.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:32.403
- [Mark] Alcohol is a big problem

00:03:32.403 --> 00:03:35.320
for a lot of American Indians, correct?

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:36.765
- Right.

00:03:36.765 --> 00:03:38.817
- [Mark] Is there a lot of
people from your community

00:03:38.817 --> 00:03:41.373
that just struggle with
that, with alcoholism?

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:43.800
- I believe so.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.260
You could tell and you can relate.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.501
And I said, I know what
that guy's going through,

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:50.168
I know what that is.

00:03:51.677 --> 00:03:52.636
I was a witness.

00:03:52.636 --> 00:03:54.230
- [Mark] Have you been through
rehabs and things like that?

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.420
- I went through rehabs,

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.650
and I basically got some information,

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.663
and it was good and
boring and bad letters,

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:08.770
it was for the best, I
think, in the long run.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.253
- [Mark] What's your go-to drink?

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:16.070
- Right now it would be
probably just a Mickey,

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:19.093
just one, depending on the weather.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:21.953
If it's too hot,

00:04:21.953 --> 00:04:22.786
I won't drink.

00:04:25.888 --> 00:04:27.400
(sighs) That's it.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.590
I'll wait till the evening
to have a couple of shots,

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.257
and I call it quits (sniffs),

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.933
from experience, eh.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.101
- [Mark] Do you ever get into,

00:04:38.101 --> 00:04:39.450
like I've heard about
people drinking things

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:44.450
like rubbing alcohol
and mouthwash and Lysol,

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.570
perfume, hairspray and
Gatorade, stuff like that.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.731
Do you ever get into any of that stuff?

00:04:49.731 --> 00:04:52.450
- Some of 'em, just to try,

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.487
just to be part of what the hell is it,

00:04:54.487 --> 00:04:55.450
and I read it.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:56.677
I read through the ingredients,

00:04:56.677 --> 00:05:00.051
and I said, "Well, look at
all the other ingredients.

00:05:00.051 --> 00:05:01.570
"This is some kind of concoction."

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:02.433
Yeah, I tried it.

00:05:03.935 --> 00:05:08.513
I've always had that daring, real bumps,

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:12.030
just go with it, just do it.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:12.880
- [Mark] You're curious?

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:13.713
- Curious.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.243
I guess that would be the word.

00:05:18.243 --> 00:05:19.860
- [Mark] Do you have children?

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.173
- I have children, yes.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.370
- [Mark] Were you around to raise them?

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:28.900
- I raised 'em till about they
were going into high school,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.143
and things went wrong.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:33.563
A big wind.

00:05:33.563 --> 00:05:34.943
A big wind came in,

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.143
and of course I was always to blame.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.193
I take it.

00:05:40.193 --> 00:05:43.013
If it was in the blame, then I take it.

00:05:44.472 --> 00:05:45.305
It was me.

00:05:45.305 --> 00:05:46.530
- [Mark] Do you still
have contact with them?

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.740
- Yeah, I try to keep in contact with 'em

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.067
as much as I can,

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:56.969
and sometimes make it "No"

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:00.264
I'm just doing this.

00:06:00.264 --> 00:06:03.470
I don't want to down nobody's day

00:06:04.712 --> 00:06:06.629
or somethin' like that,

00:06:08.273 --> 00:06:10.573
but they live not too
far from here (mumbles),

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.893
and they're beautiful children, healthy.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.400
- [Mark] In looking back at your life,

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.690
do you have any regrets?

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.613
Do you have any things
you wish you had done,

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.510
wish you hadn't done?

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:27.283
- Well, wishing I hadn't done,

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.500
I can let that, it's nothin',

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:31.333
I just let it go.

00:06:31.333 --> 00:06:32.283
That's already gone.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.813
What I wanted to do and didn't become,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.760
that's irrelevant, I don't need it.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:40.840
I don't even want it,

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.033
but I have no regrets.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.820
I had a beautiful life.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.507
It was good and bad and vice a versa.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.917
- [Mark] How old are you today?

00:06:53.917 --> 00:06:55.387
- I'm 58.
- 58.

00:06:56.746 --> 00:07:00.570
- 58 years old, I was born in 1960

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:03.811
in Fort Defiance, Gallup, I mean-

00:07:03.811 --> 00:07:07.599
- [Mark] Born in '60
would make you 59 or 60.

00:07:07.599 --> 00:07:10.643
- (chuckling) Fort Defiance,
Arizona, is where I was born.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.840
- [Mark] What do you do for money now?

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:14.940
- What I do for money?

00:07:16.224 --> 00:07:18.710
Well, I do anything that I would try

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:21.483
to make an honest money,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.884
and nowadays I can't even,

00:07:26.884 --> 00:07:29.420
I don't even feel like locating

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.410
or even trying to write up a resume

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.713
or look over my blueprint.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.823
I leave it as I lost interest.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.190
- [Mark] You've given up hope?

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:41.407
- I lost interest, like,

00:07:41.407 --> 00:07:43.137
"Man, I live it the way I want to live it.

00:07:43.137 --> 00:07:45.504
"If I'm still alive, I'm alive."

00:07:45.504 --> 00:07:46.820
"If I fuck that, if I have to,"

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:47.653
excuse my language,

00:07:47.653 --> 00:07:49.417
"but if I have to eat
out of the garbage can,

00:07:49.417 --> 00:07:50.250
"I'll do it.

00:07:51.607 --> 00:07:53.177
"No pun off me."

00:07:55.797 --> 00:07:58.407
If they don't understand that (grumbles)

00:07:58.407 --> 00:07:59.265
maybe they don't.

00:07:59.265 --> 00:08:01.680
- [Mark] What was the hardest
part of your life now?

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.928
- [Vern] Probably when I was younger,

00:08:03.928 --> 00:08:06.378
when I didn't quite
understand what was going on.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.030
What I mean by what's going on

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:16.730
is it's me, you know.

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:19.260
How come mom and dad go and break apart?

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:22.110
What's going on?

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:23.411
- [Mark] Did your parents split up?

00:08:23.411 --> 00:08:24.597
- [Vern] I was doing my hair (mumbles).

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:41.030
- [Mark] In the winter, it
gets very cold out here.

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:43.570
How do you survive the cold winters?

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:48.570
- [Vern] With extra material

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:57.020
and basically wool, and
you'll be all right.

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:00.978
- [Mark] Do you see
people dying in the cold?

00:09:00.978 --> 00:09:02.240
- [Vern] It's like, "Geez,"

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.702
I went to move 'em

00:09:03.702 --> 00:09:05.623
and I tell 'em, "Hey, what's up man?

00:09:05.623 --> 00:09:08.822
"Hey get up, man, you're gonna freeze.

00:09:08.822 --> 00:09:09.655
"Want me to call in for you?

00:09:09.655 --> 00:09:10.488
"We'll call.

00:09:10.488 --> 00:09:12.022
"Someone will call in for ya.

00:09:12.022 --> 00:09:13.200
"I will or they will."

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:14.768
- [Mark] But that happens quite a bit?

00:09:14.768 --> 00:09:16.800
- [Vern] Yeah.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.340
- [Mark] Are you proud of
your heritage of being Navajo?

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:25.270
- Hmm, I never gave it quite
deep thought about that.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:26.210
I don't get all

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:31.563
bang ho on that stuff.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:35.060
I don't even look at it as

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:44.783
that kind of a form or statement.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.050
I look at it as,

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:52.050
Oh, I'm basically just a
native young boy turned man,

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:57.773
still want to learn,

00:09:59.068 --> 00:10:00.270
still want to know things

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.840
and appreciative when I find out

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.243
about things that are
interesting I never heard.

00:10:08.187 --> 00:10:09.457
And that's what makes...

00:10:11.549 --> 00:10:12.850
- [Mark] If you could
live your life over again,

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:14.203
what would you do differently?

00:10:16.169 --> 00:10:19.270
- (clears throat) What
would I do most differently?

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.050
Well, man, I say,

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:23.997
I would like to just (chuckles)

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:33.100
just be basic, get down to basic

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.167
and take care of everything from A to B.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:40.220
If I have an appointment, hit it.

00:10:42.221 --> 00:10:45.740
If I gotta go get my ID, get it.

00:10:46.649 --> 00:10:50.270
If I gotta reapply something, I'll go.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:51.260
- [Mark] Just being more responsible?

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:54.210
- Being more responsible would
be the word, yes. Thank you.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:57.990
Yeah, I'd like to be more responsible.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:10:59.640
Nowadays, I'm just laid back.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:00.473
I just gave up everything.

00:11:00.473 --> 00:11:03.183
I don't even want to hear the
radio or the news or nothing.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:07.633
Yeah.

00:11:07.633 --> 00:11:09.260
- [Mark] You just want
people to leave you alone?

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:12.313
- Yeah, I just basically
wanna get away from everybody,

00:11:14.461 --> 00:11:16.164
and I knew they have problems

00:11:16.164 --> 00:11:17.845
and I just sit there and I listen,

00:11:17.845 --> 00:11:19.012
I talk to 'em.

00:11:20.542 --> 00:11:24.320
And (mumbles) get a controversy and then,

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.710
yeah, we have somethin' in common.

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:31.650
We all have somethin' that's blocking

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:34.663
that past door into family.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.577
There's always gotta be one that blocks it

00:11:37.577 --> 00:11:39.707
always saying, "You ain't family."

00:11:39.707 --> 00:11:40.860
"Shut up, man.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:42.977
"Get out of the way.

00:11:42.977 --> 00:11:47.977
"We're family. You just don't
know it, understand it."

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:51.117
Basically that's right,

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.860
but I have my mother, she's still alive.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.170
God bless her.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:57.150
- [Mark] You see your mom still?

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:58.880
- I'm ready to go see her though.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:01.420
I'll call her, but I don't have a phone.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:04.187
I have a phone but the
(indistinct) has the phone,

00:12:04.187 --> 00:12:05.763
but I have no battery.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:07.800
I don't have it no.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:11.003
I gotta get me a SIM card.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.763
I got a charger, but yeah.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:21.473
So basically we've been camping
out for about like, a while,

00:12:24.983 --> 00:12:28.673
over two months, in a certain area.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:33.690
And I move up and I run
into some people that I know

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:35.603
that are relatives I didn't even know.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:39.330
So it's a small world (chuckles).

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:41.663
- [Mark] All right, Vern.

00:12:41.663 --> 00:12:43.321
Well, thank you so much
for sharing your story,

00:12:43.321 --> 00:12:44.521
and good luck out there.

00:12:45.663 --> 00:12:48.230
I hope you get back
together with your family.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:49.680
- I do too.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.110
I know they're doing good.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:52.862
And I don't want, like I said,

00:12:52.862 --> 00:12:54.838
I don't wanna down 'em.

00:12:54.838 --> 00:12:56.673
But I'd like to say hello.

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:02.293
I love you, just wanna see what's up,

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:05.630
miss your home cookin',

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:07.180
stuff like that.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:08.423
That's what I'd like.

00:13:09.577 --> 00:13:11.613
But then again, I got to get going.

00:13:13.145 --> 00:13:14.270
What I'm searching for?

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:15.103
I don't know.

00:13:16.824 --> 00:13:17.800
Somethin' I like, though.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:21.043
Somethin' I really enjoy and
I just can't find it here.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.320
Yeah, occupation habit or hobby.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:28.160
A hobby.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.990
- [Mark] It's good to
have something you love.

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:31.840
- Mm-hmm (affirmative).

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:33.400
- [Mark] Vern, thank you very much.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:34.831
- Okay. Thank you.

00:13:34.831 --> 00:13:37.130
And can you straight?

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:37.963
Thank you.

